<% survey = @show.open_endedness_survey %>
<% cost = survey.bet("energy-per-epoch") %>
<% structure = survey.bet("environmental-structure") %>
<% room = survey.bet("max-tape-len") %>

<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  <%= link_to "On the substrate the programme already had", finding_path("replicator-complexity-plateau") %>,
  the dominant replicator's complexity is counted after emergence and the count is of
  directions: how many transitioned worlds end more complicated than they started. That is
  the baseline. The open question it leaves is whether the substrate was what was limiting —
  whether a world that has to pay for computation, a world whose regions differ, or a world
  whose tapes are free to lengthen goes somewhere the default substrate never went. This
  page is that verdict, for the three sweeps DESIGN §1.3 built to ask it: sweep 6
  (instruction cost), sweep 7 (environmental structure) and sweep 8 (room to grow).
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  Each of the three sweeps carries its own control. The first arm of each grid is the
  substrate every earlier sweep ran — <span class="mono">energy_per_epoch</span> 0, a
  <span class="mono">uniform</span> world, <span class="mono">max_tape_len</span> equal to
  the tape length — so a treated arm is read against the default substrate inside the same
  experiment, at the same mutation rate, world size and step budget, rather than against
  runs from another sweep.
</p>
<p>
  For every transitioned run of those sweeps this reads two series off the stored samples at
  or after that run's own crossing epoch: <span class="mono">dominant_compressed_len</span>,
  the compressed length of the most populous tape that passes the replicator test, and
  <span class="mono">distinct_lineages</span>, how many lineage ids the cells hold. A run's
  <strong>plateau</strong> is the highest reading its samples record after the crossing —
  the level it settled at, not a fitted trend — and a run is <strong>still rising</strong>
  when its last reading is above its first. An arm is then placed by counting: a treated arm
  raises the plateau when more than half of its measured runs sit strictly above the median
  plateau of the control arm's runs. Nothing on this page is fitted, and nothing is
  averaged across sweeps.
</p>
<p>
  A verdict needs
  <%= pluralize Findings::OpenEndednessSurvey::MIN_ARM_RUNS, "measured run" %> in the control
  arm and in at least one other arm of the same sweep. Below that the hypothesis reads
  <strong>unresolved</strong> with the counts behind that: an arm nothing transitioned in
  refutes nothing.
</p>

<h2>Hypothesis 1 — instruction cost</h2>
<p>
  DESIGN §1.3 sweep 6: <em>a cost pressure selects for efficient copiers and opens a second
  niche.</em> A second niche is a second way of making a living, and what this substrate
  records of that is the lineages it keeps alive, so the priced arms are read against the
  costless one on <span class="mono">distinct_lineages</span>.
</p>
<%= render "findings/substrate_verdict", bet: cost, reading: "a lineage count", unit: "lineages" %>

<h2>Hypothesis 2 — environmental structure</h2>
<p>
  DESIGN §1.3 sweep 7: <em>environmental structure raises the plateau the dominant
  replicator's complexity settles at, refuted if a world whose regions differ plateaus where
  a uniform world does.</em>
</p>
<%= render "findings/substrate_verdict", bet: structure, reading: "a complexity in bytes", unit: "bytes" %>
<p>
  Its secondary prediction — <em>a heterogeneous world keeps more lineages alive after
  emergence</em> — is the same arms read on <span class="mono">distinct_lineages</span>.
</p>
<%= render "findings/substrate_verdict", bet: structure.with(decided_by: :lineages),
           reading: "a lineage count", unit: "lineages" %>

<h2>Hypothesis 3 — room to grow</h2>
<p>
  DESIGN §1.3 sweep 8: <em>room to grow raises the plateau the dominant replicator's
  complexity settles at, refuted if tapes free to lengthen plateau where fixed-length tapes
  do</em> — which would say the 64-byte ceiling was never the binding constraint.
</p>
<%= render "findings/substrate_verdict", bet: room, reading: "a complexity in bytes", unit: "bytes" %>

<h2>Does anything keep rising?</h2>
<% if survey.any? %>
  <p>
    Across the three sweeps the lab holds
    <%= pluralize survey.transitioned_count, "transitioned run" %>.
    <%= survey.measured_count(:length) %> of them carry a complexity reading after their
    crossing and <%= survey.rising_count(:length) %> end above their first;
    <%= survey.measured_count(:lineages) %> carry a lineage count and
    <%= survey.rising_count(:lineages) %> end above their first. Rising here is one run's
    last sample against its first, censored at both ends by that run's budget — a world
    still climbing when its epochs ran out reads as rising, and one that would have climbed
    later reads as flat.
  </p>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No run of the three substrate sweeps has transitioned in this database yet, so all three
    hypotheses are unresolved and nothing above is a result. The sweeps are queued; this page
    reads them at render time and fills in as their runs cross.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>What this does not claim</h2>
<p>
  <strong>A plateau is a plateau at these budgets.</strong> Every arm runs the same 20 000
  epochs as the sweeps it is compared to, and a run's series is cut off wherever its budget
  ended. A substrate that would have elaborated at epoch 200 000 reads flat here, and the
  refutation condition DESIGN states is met at this budget rather than in general.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Counts, not effect sizes.</strong> A majority of an arm's runs above the control
  arm's median is a direction, not a magnitude, and at the seed counts these sweeps carry a
  direction is compatible with a large range of true effects. None of the three verdicts is
  a significance test.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Complexity is size and lineages are descent.</strong> Compressed length bounds the
  recipe rather than describing it, and
  <span class="mono">distinct_lineages</span> counts descent rather than shape — two
  lineages that drifted onto the same tape still count twice. Neither reading opens a tape
  and says what it does, so "a second niche" is inferred from a count of lineages rather
  than observed.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>One reading per sweep decides it.</strong> Each hypothesis is judged on the
  observable it names and nothing else. An arm that raises the complexity plateau while
  losing lineages would read supported on sweep 7's primary prediction and not supported on
  its secondary one, which is two sentences about the same world rather than a contradiction.
</p>

<h2>What next</h2>
<p>
  If every arm plateaus where its control does, the three substrate bets are spent and the
  design record's rung 4 needs a richer substrate rather than a wider grid — and this page is
  the evidence for saying so. If an arm does raise the plateau, the next run is that arm
  alone at a budget long enough to see whether the new level is itself a plateau. Either way
  the reading that would settle it is per lineage rather than per dominant tape, so one
  colony can be watched elaborating instead of a population being scored by whichever tape
  is most populous at each sample.
</p>
